title a recovery lap is an ordinary lap
prompt the standing fleet baton -- take the next agent-doable lap in the mantra and tally lane
rota lap 4188, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; its fixed seat, the happy zone and the thin edge, asks for two small families of checks at every seam and an explicit contract between them. Read up close, as taste asks: the seam between mantra's weave and its store has no contract at all, because the program that persists a weave reimplements both sides privately. That reading became this lap's named crux rather than its work.
think THE MECHANISM, PLAINLY. Seven files came out of stash@{0} with `git checkout stash@{0} -- <path>`, which reads the TREE object and so carried mode through, where `git show stash@{0}:<path> >` would have landed them 100644 (the exec-bit law's own clause). Four are the parked lane work -- mantra/src/diff.rye, mantra/src/diff_witness.rye, tools/m/mantra_diff_witness.rish, tools/fixtures/m/mantra_diff_control.sh -- and two are session logs. The archive shelf was read out of the stash to /tmp, renumbered, and written fresh; the four living pins the stash also touched were NOT restored, because peers have moved every one of them since the stash's base.
think The cold roster opened with four reds and I read the evidence rather than the counts. standing_equipment was red because three others were, which its own `red_self` line says. readme_reach named ONE living broken link and reds_fold failed at the line that runs readme_reach's scan, so two guards were reporting one fault.
think That fault was on the SHARED card: construction/ITINERARY.md cited archive/REDS-a-label-is-not-its-target-rows-571.md, and that row folded to rows-570 -- 571 is the pen row's shelf, one door along. Before calling it broken I ran `sh tools/fixtures/p/path_absence_scan.sh`, which fetches xy first and answers absent-or-behind per path: here=no upstream=no, verdict=absent (%457's instrument, used for what it was built for). Repointed.
think stash_record read unlanded=2 and `sh tools/fixtures/s/stash_record_scan.sh list` named both paths on their own lines. Both are this lane's, and they are a chain: 20260907-111425 closed the diff.rye fence, and 20260907-132732 recovered THAT lap out of the previous stash. The recovery lap was parked by the next round-open before it could ship.
think So reds-first chose the lap again, one turn further in than last time: recover and land, and say plainly why a recovery is not safe from the thing it recovers from.
obs A RECOVERY LAP IS AN ORDINARY LAP. `fleet_round_open.sh` stashes a dirty tree at the open, with no notion of what the work is, so a lap that exists to rescue parked work is parked by exactly the rule that parked the work. Nothing here is broken: stash_record saw it, named the paths, and reddened until a hand came. What it costs is that the SAME work can be lost twice, and the count of round-opens between writing and landing is unbounded. This is %499's family read from the inside -- a park keeps every byte and still costs the lap -- and it is Keaton's, since six ships run that file.
obs The number moved a third time and the stamp never did. The row booked %561, was renumbered to %570 when a peer published %561, and is %572 now because a peer published %570 while the recovery sat parked. `sh tools/fixtures/r/reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next` after `git fetch xy` answered 572 both times I asked. Each renumbering touched the shelf filename, its two internal headings, the recital line, the pin headline and two log `file` lines -- and nothing else, because every living citation already spelled 20260907.111135. That is derived-spine rule 4 paying for itself twice on one row.
obs The shelf's own Folded stamp moved to this lap's 20260907.144347 rather than keeping the parked lap's 20260907.132334, because a fold that never landed did not happen, and the recital line has to agree with it.
obs Both recovered logs were edited before landing, which is lawful because an unlanded log is still being written -- the 132732 log's own precedent. The edits are narrow: the two `file` lines that name the shelf path, so a named path is a path that exists, and one parenthetical inside an obs recording that the log was itself parked and the number moved again. Nothing about what the parked laps actually did was rewritten.
obs A GUARD REDDENED ON ITS OWN MODULES BEING HONEST. tools/w/width_check_th3.rish greps mantra/src/*.rye for `usize` and subtracts the two cast forms, so it counted the `// usize lives at the inherited-std seam alone` comments that stand beside every cast to explain WHY the seam is there -- 7 hits across diff.rye and weave.rye, all prose, zero authored widths. It reds at HEAD too and it is UNROSTERED, so nothing in the tree has heard it. The scan drops comment-only lines now (`grep -v '^[[:space:]]*//'` ahead of the usize grep), and a declaration carrying a trailing comment still reads, since only the leading forms are cut.
obs That fix is proven BOTH WAYS on metal rather than in the passing direction alone: `const scratch_probe: usize = 0;` appended to mantra/src/store.rye reds the witness, and removing it returns it to GREEN with store.rye byte-identical. A guard watched only while it passes cannot be told from a bypass.
obs NOT ROSTERED, AND I DID NOT ROSTER IT. width_check_th3 gates a real law on four real files, and rostering it is a one-line edit -- yet a guard that has been red at HEAD for an unknown span deserves its own lap to establish it stays green across the tree it claims, rather than riding in on a recovery lap. Named here so the next lap can take it.
obs MY OWN SLIP, THE SAME ONE THIS LANE RECORDED THREE HOURS AGO. To ask whether width_check_th3 was red at HEAD I ran `git stash push -u` and popped it back, exactly the shape the 132732 log booked against itself. The pop succeeded and nothing was lost, and it was still wrong: `git show HEAD:<path>` and a throwaway worktree answer the same question without moving the tree at all. A lantern that fires twice should become a loom, and this one has now fired twice in one lane in one day.
obs THE LANE'S CRUX, MEASURED AND NOT TAKEN. mantra/src/weave.rye is 653 lines with LineId{pos,site}, merge, annotate, a head scan holding the head to the module, and six rostered guards -- and `grep -rn '@import("weave.rye")'` finds it imported by its own witnesses and by diff.rye alone. mantra/src/main.rye is 713 lines, is what `rye build mantra/src/main.rye` produces, is run by tools/r/rw1_history_contract.rish and tools/s/slc1_accept.rish, and imports std and parse_int.rye and nothing else: it redeclares Line, Diff, Weave and Store privately, computes its own diff, and serializes `mantra-weave-v1` as `{gen}\t{pos}\t{text}`. There is no column for the site, so every weave this tree has ever stored names its lines by half an identity -- the half a per-weave counter makes ambiguous the moment two weaves part.
obs What that costs the charter: active-designing/20260905-153729 orders the arc line, weave, showing, SEAM, reading, and its seam movement -- the weave meets Tablecloth by content -- assumed the published weave was the running one. It is not. The first rung is a wire form on weave.rye itself: encode and decode over a v2 carrying the whole pair, length-prefixed so a line holding a tab or a newline round-trips (which v1 asserts against, since both are its separators), canonical so one weave has one content name, proven on a MERGED weave, which is the case v1 cannot represent at all. It is drafted and not landed: this lap's reds came first and a recovery is not the place to open a movement.
obs Not booked as a red, and why: the repair is main.rye adopting the published modules, which refactors a shipping binary under a live acceptance witness, and that is a lap with a word on it rather than a mechanical one. The finding stands on the card where Keaton reads it.
